    {
      "slide_id": "masterclass-1",
      "slide_role": "masterclass",
      "background_kind": "curated",
      "heading": "1 · Generate, then select",
      "sentence": "Creativity needs both exploration and systematic evaluation — schedule both, never in the same minute.",
      "quote": "The first stage is to make the map. The second stage is to choose a route on the map.",
      "quote_origin": "page_verified",
      "citation": {
        "label": "(de Bono 1985, 199)",
        "href": "/creativity-techniques-uem/lessons/en/creativity-techniques/u-2-idea-generation-selection/#ref-debono-1985"
      },
      "semantic_tags": {
        "themes": [
          "generation-selection"
        ],
        "cidoc": [
          "crm:E33_Linguistic_Object"
        ],
        "getty_aat": [
          "creativity (concept)"
        ],
        "dcterms": [
          "dcterms:type=Text",
          "dcterms:subject=generation-selection"
        ]
      },
      "image_brief": "Concept-art sheet for the Blender Studio short Sprite Fright (2021): many labelled variants of one character's head rendered side by side before one is chosen — generate first, then select.",
      "asset_id": "wikimedia:File:Sprite Fright-concept art-Victoria 01.png",
      "media_slot_id": "U2.masterclass-1",
      "notes": "- Generation and selection are two different jobs. Doing them in the same minute makes both weaker.\n- That is why the Lab runs them as two exercises: silent brainwriting first, selection second.\n- Honest audit: was your last piece one first idea polished, or one of several considered on purpose?\n- Some briefs really do need the first answer. When time is the constraint, generate-then-select is a luxury.\n- Judging too early has a cost: students who expected their artwork to be evaluated produced work judged less creative.\n- One small lab study: open-monitoring meditation helped a divergent-thinking task.\nSource: the map/route quote is (de Bono 1985, 199); one creative act holds both divergent and convergent thinking is (Craft 2000, 30); the evaluation result is (Amabile 1979, 221); the meditation study is (Colzato, Ozturk, and Hommel 2012, 1). Other bullets are course wording.\nAsk: Where does the line fall between \"take the first answer\" and \"generate more\"?",
      "claims": [
        {
          "text": "The first stage is to make the map. The second stage is to choose a route on the map.",
          "cite": "de Bono 1985, 199"
        },
        {
          "text": "subjects in the evaluation groups produced artworks significantly lower on judged creativity than did subjects in the nonevaluation control groups",
          "cite": "Amabile 1979, 221"
        },
        {
          "text": "OM meditation induces a control state that promotes divergent thinking ... FA meditation does not sustain convergent thinking",
          "cite": "Colzato, Ozturk, and Hommel 2012, 1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "slide_id": "masterclass-2",
      "slide_role": "masterclass",
      "background_kind": "diagram",
      "heading": "2 · Fluency with flexibility",
      "sentence": "Many ideas only help if they differ. Thirty sketches of the same sun are many ideas, but only one kind.",
      "quote": "It involves fluency, or the ability to generate a great quantity of ideas; flexibility, or the ability to switch from one perspective to another; and originality in picking unusual associations of ideas.",
      "quote_origin": "page_verified",
      "semantic_tags": {
        "themes": [
          "fluency",
          "flexibility"
        ],
        "cidoc": [
          "crm:E33_Linguistic_Object"
        ],
        "getty_aat": [
          "creativity (concept)"
        ],
        "dcterms": [
          "dcterms:type=Text",
          "dcterms:subject=fluency"
        ]
      },
      "citation": {
        "label": "(Csikszentmihalyi 1996, chap. 3)",
        "href": "/creativity-techniques-uem/lessons/en/creativity-techniques/u-2-idea-generation-selection/#ref-csikszentmihalyi-1996"
      },
      "image_brief": "Leonardo da Vinci, a Codex Atlanticus sheet of machine studies, c. 1490s: ideas that differ in kind, not thirty versions of one — fluency only counts when it comes with flexibility.",
      "notes": "- In the Lab you count both: how many ideas (fluency) and how many kinds (flexibility).\n- Someone has to decide whether two passages are different possibilities or variations of one.\n- Selection follows the period of production; it does not interrupt it.\nSource: the slide quote is (Csikszentmihalyi 1996, chap. 3); \"Lateral thinking is generative. Vertical thinking is selective.\" is (de Bono 1970, introduction). Other bullets are course wording.\nAsk: How do you decide if two ideas are different, or one idea twice?",
      "claims": [
        {
          "text": "It involves fluency, or the ability to generate a great quantity of ideas; flexibility, or the ability to switch from one perspective to another; and originality in picking unusual associations of ideas.",
          "cite": "Csikszentmihalyi 1996, chap. 3"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "slide_id": "masterclass-3",
      "slide_role": "masterclass",
      "background_kind": "curated",
      "heading": "3 · Get past the obvious",
      "sentence": "Your first ideas are often the obvious ones. In this Lab, don't censor them — keep making more without judging, and dig in a different place.",
      "quote": "You cannot dig a hole in a different place by digging the same hole deeper.",
      "quote_origin": "page_verified",
      "semantic_tags": {
        "themes": [
          "lateral-thinking",
          "obvious-answer"
        ],
        "cidoc": [
          "crm:E33_Linguistic_Object"
        ],
        "getty_aat": [
          "creativity (concept)"
        ],
        "dcterms": [
          "dcterms:type=Text",
          "dcterms:subject=lateral-thinking"
        ]
      },
      "citation": {
        "label": "(de Bono 1970, introduction)",
        "href": "/creativity-techniques-uem/lessons/en/creativity-techniques/u-2-idea-generation-selection/#ref-debono-1970"
      },
      "image_brief": "A brainstorming wall of sticky notes (2014): early ideas kept on the wall without judging, so that less obvious ones have room to appear.",
      "asset_id": "wikimedia:File:Stickies to brainstorm Edit.2014..jpg",
      "media_slot_id": "U2.masterclass-3",
      "notes": "- First ideas are often the obvious ones, and they are sticky: designers tend to \"hang on to their principal solution concept\", and a fixation effect, suggested by Jansson and Smith, makes designers shown an example copy its features.\n- \"Generate numerous ideas. It is dangerous to become fixated upon one or two ideas too early in the process.\"\n- Osborn's first rule: \"Criticism must be withheld until all ideas are in.\"\n- In this Lab: produce more without judging instead of censoring; brainwrite in silence, then come back in Exercise 2 as a selector. On your D1, count how many ideas you wrote before one felt neither obvious nor random.\nSource: the dig-hole quote is (de Bono 1970, introduction); stickiness and fixation are (Cross 2006, 81–82); \"Generate numerous ideas…\" is (Norman 2013, 226); the rule is (Osborn 1942, chap. 4); looking for alternatives instead of accepting the most obvious approach is (de Bono 1970, chap. “The Generation of Alternatives”). The Lab bullet is course wording.\nAsk: How many ideas are enough for a given brief? The lesson leaves this open.",
      "claims": [
        {
          "text": "You cannot dig a hole in a different place by digging the same hole deeper.",
          "cite": "de Bono 1970, introduction"
        },
        {
          "text": "Lateral thinking is not a substitute for vertical thinking. Both are required They are complementary.",
          "cite": "de Bono 1970, introduction"
        },
        {
          "text": "they appear to hang on to their principal solution concept",
          "cite": "Cross 2006, 81–82"
        },
        {
          "text": "Generate numerous ideas. It is dangerous to become fixated upon one or two ideas too early in the process.",
          "cite": "Norman 2013, 226"
        },
        {
          "text": "Judicial judgment is ruled out. Criticism must be withheld until all ideas are in.",
          "cite": "Osborn 1942, chap. 4"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "slide_id": "masterclass-4",
      "slide_role": "masterclass",
      "background_kind": "curated",
      "heading": "4 · Role and constraint tools",
      "sentence": "Hats and cards stage a cognitive move — map the kinds of thinking first, then choose a route.",
      "quote": "Any tool should be useful in the expected way, but a truly great tool lends itself to uses you never expected.",
      "quote_origin": "page_verified",
      "citation": {
        "label": "(Raymond 2001, 44)",
        "href": "/creativity-techniques-uem/lessons/en/creativity-techniques/u-2-idea-generation-selection/#ref-raymond-2001"
      },
      "semantic_tags": {
        "themes": [
          "provocation",
          "six-hats"
        ],
        "cidoc": [
          "crm:E33_Linguistic_Object"
        ],
        "getty_aat": [
          "creativity (concept)"
        ],
        "dcterms": [
          "dcterms:type=Text",
          "dcterms:subject=provocation"
        ]
      },
      "image_brief": "A Six Thinking Hats session board for the black hat ('doubts': what are the risks?), Wikimedia Colombia workshop, 2025: a role tool that stages one mode of thinking at a time.",
      "asset_id": "wikimedia:File:Sombrero negro (Dudas).jpg",
      "media_slot_id": "U2.masterclass-4",
      "notes": "- The studio is never neutral: the first voice can narrow the room. Role and constraint tools disturb that frame.\n- Six Thinking Hats: one mode at a time. White facts, red feelings, black caution, yellow benefits, green new ideas, blue running the process.\n- An Oblique Strategies card gives one short prompt for changing a D1 object. It is a deck of prompts, not a theory or a guarantee.\n- de Bono: lateral thinking is provocative, \"a way of bringing about repatterning\". What-if thinking widens the alternatives you consider, but it can hurt the generation of new ideas.\nSource: the tool quote is (Raymond 2001, 44); provocation and repatterning are (de Bono 1970, chap. “Difference between Lateral and Vertical Thinking”); what-if thinking is (Wong, Galinsky, and Kray 2009, 161, 168). The Hats and Oblique Strategies bullets are course wording (the lesson names the hats without a page cite).\nAsk: Who speaks first in your group, and what does that close?",
      "claims": [
        {
          "text": "Any tool should be useful in the expected way, but a truly great tool lends itself to uses you never expected.",
          "cite": "Raymond 2001, 44"
